{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"66ace335-c30e-4157-8b27-5f80e904f0f0","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Draw the block on a slope at angle theta. A free-body diagram will show forces acting on the block, not its motion.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Block on an incline"},{"op":"figure","id":"incline","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3.5,3.5],"y":[-2.1,2.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"slope","from":[-3,-1.732],"to":[3,1.732]},{"kind":"segment","from":[-3,-1.732],"to":[-1.2,-1.732],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"angle","at":[-3,-1.732],"from":[-1.2,-1.732],"to":[3,1.732],"r":0.2,"label":"θ"},{"kind":"polyline","id":"block","points":[[-0.606,-0.35],[0.606,0.35],[0.106,1.216],[-1.106,0.516]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[-0.25,0.433]}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"66ace335-c30e-4157-8b27-5f80e904f0f0","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Weight points vertically downward. The surface pushes perpendicular to the incline with a normal force, not vertically upward.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"forces","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"weight","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-0.25,-1.567],"arrow":true,"label":"mg"},{"kind":"segment","id":"normal","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-1.116,1.933],"arrow":true,"label":"N"}]},{"op":"text","id":"directions","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Resolve forces along and across the slope."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"66ace335-c30e-4157-8b27-5f80e904f0f0","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Resolve weight into downhill and inward components. These dashed arrows replace weight in calculations; they are not two additional forces.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"components","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"parallel","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[-1.116,-0.067],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg sin θ"},{"kind":"segment","id":"inward","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[0.616,-1.067],"arrow":true,"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg cos θ"},{"kind":"segment","from":[-1.116,-0.067],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[0.616,-1.067],"to":{"on":"weight","t":1},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"resolve","anchor":{"el":"directions","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"W_{\\parallel}=mg\\sin\\theta,\\qquad W_{\\perp}=mg\\cos\\theta"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Which component of weight tends to slide the block downhill?","expected_gist":"mg sin θ, parallel to the incline"}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"66ace335-c30e-4157-8b27-5f80e904f0f0","step":{"id":"s4","script":"If static friction holds the block at rest, it points uphill. Normal force balances the inward component, while friction balances the downhill component.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"friction","on":"incline","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"static","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[0.616,0.933],"arrow":true,"label":"fₛ"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"balance","anchor":{"el":"resolve","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\text{At rest:}\\quad N=mg\\cos\\theta,\\quad f_s=mg\\sin\\theta"},{"op":"equation","id":"limit","anchor":{"el":"balance","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f_s\\leq\\mu_sN\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\tan\\theta\\leq\\mu_s"}],"checkpoint":null}}
